# HTB: Redeemer (redis) — Write-up

**Difficulty:** Easy

## Overview

Redeemer is designed to teach the basics of enumerating and interacting with a Redis database. The goal is a single flag retrieved from an unauthenticated Redis instance with no password configured.

## Environment Setup

1. Virtualbox with kali linux setup and connected to an openvpn to the target
2. Opened a terminal within kali to begin enumeration.

## Step 1 — Connectivity Check

Before scanning, confirmed the target was reachable:

```bash
ping 10.129.198.237
```

Received replies, confirming the host was up and network connectivity to the target was working.

## Step 2 — Port Scanning

Ran an Nmap service scan against the target to identify open ports and running services:

```bash
nmap -p- -sV -T5 10.129.198.237
```
- `-p-`  scan all 65,535 TCP ports, instead of just the default top 1,000.useful when a default scan comes back empty or you suspect a service is running on a nonstandard port.
- `-sV` enables version detection, so Nmap reports the service and version running on each open port.
- `-T5`  timing template, controls scan speed/aggressiveness. Templates range from -T0 (paranoid, very slow) to -T5 (insane, fastest possible). -T5 sends probes rapidly with minimal delay between them
**Result:** Port `6379/tcp` (Redis) was open — the only port exposed on the host.

![Flag](Redeemer_1.png)

## Step 3 — Service Access

Redis is an in-memory key-value database. By default, it does not require authentication unless explicitly configured with a password (`requirepass`).
```bash
redis-cli -h 
```
![Flag](Redeemer_2.png)
- `-h` points the client at the target host instead of the local default.

The connection succeeded immediately with no credentials required, dropping into an interactive Redis prompt.

## Step 4 — Enumerating the Database

Ran the `INFO` command to pull server metadata and statistics:

```
INFO
```

The `Keyspace` section of the output showed a single logical database at index `0`. Selected it:

```
SELECT 0
```

Listed all keys stored inside:

```
KEYS *
```
![Flag](Redeemer_3.png)
This revealed a key holding the flag data.

## Step 5 — Retrieving the Flag

Read the value of the identified key:

```
GET flag
```

This returned the flag, completing the challenge.

## Summary

| Step | Action | Finding |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `ping` | Host is up |
| 2 | `nmap -sV` | Port 6379 (Redis) open |
| 3 | `redis-cli -h <target-ip>` | Unauthenticated access, no password required |
| 4 | `INFO` / `SELECT 0` / `KEYS *` | Located key holding the flag |
| 5 | `GET <key_name>` | Flag retrieved |

## Takeaways

- Redis has no authentication enabled by default — an exposed, unconfigured instance allows full read/write access to anyone who can reach it on the network.
- `INFO`, `SELECT`, and `KEYS *` alone are enough to fully enumerate an unprotected Redis database.